# Logs
*.log

# Item-collection files for temporary testing.  (These were one fused line,
# `*.tsv*.epub.sha256` — a pattern matching nothing — which is why both kinds
# kept surfacing as untracked.)
*.tsv
*.epub.sha256

# Corpus fingerprint for the viewer article-cache bust — build output, a hash of
# data/derived/articles (which is itself untracked).  A committed copy could not be
# verified from the repo and would go stale against the local corpus; deploy.sh
# regenerates it immediately before upload.
tools/viewer/build-stamp.js

# EPUBCheck, the TEI Consortium-independent validator we gate the books on.
# 32 MB of third-party jar — installed, not committed.  `tools/check_epub.sh`
# IS committed, knows this path, and prints the download URL when the jar is
# absent: the pair is what makes the tool findable from a fresh clone, which a
# jar sitting in a session scratchpad was not.
tools/epubcheck/

# The built books, their published checksums, their provenance sidecars, and the
# build logs.  `*.epub` already ignored the files; this ignores the DIRECTORY,
# which is the thing deploy.sh now mkdir -p's.  ~800 MB of dead July probe builds
# used to sit loose in the repo root beside them; the two books we ship are all
# that is left.
#
# ANCHORED, and it has to be.  Written as `epub/` this matched a directory of
# that name at ANY depth — including `src/britannica/epub/`, the SOURCE package.
# Its nine files stayed in git only because they predate the rule, and git keeps
# tracking what it already tracks; a NEW module added there vanished silently,
# never appearing in `git status` at all.  `/mdx/` below was anchored from the
# start.  (`git check-ignore` will not warn you: it does not report tracked files
# as ignored unless you pass `--no-index`, so the rule reads as harmless.)
/epub/

# MDX dictionaries, staging files and isolated reader QA installation.
/mdx/
